WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:17.830
- Hi everyone, welcome to the
daily home room livestream,

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:19.800
which is something that
all of us at Khan Academy

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.990
started up once we started
having mass school,

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:25.100
physical closures, I should say.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.180
Many, now it seems like a lifetime ago,

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.410
it's just a way to keep in touch,

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.820
have interesting
conversations, and understand

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.240
what's happening in the world around us

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.220
in education and beyond.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.730
Before we get into the conversation

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.170
with a very exciting guest
today, I always give the reminder

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.670
to folks that Khan Academy is
a not-for-profit organization

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:44.980
with the mission of providing
free world class education

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.240
for anyone, anywhere.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.610
And we exist because of
philanthropic donations.

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:49.940
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:51.420
please think about donating.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.380
I wanna give a special
shout out to several

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.110
corporate partners who've stepped up,

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.570
especially because of our COVID response.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.810
You could imagine, our
traffic is almost three times

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.580
what it typically is,
our costs are going up,

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.410
we're trying to put
more programs out there

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:04.480
sooner than later.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.360
And so special thanks to Bank of America,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:11.320
Google.org, Novartis, AT&amp;T,
Fastly for stepping up,

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.540
and for the many other supporters

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:13.640
for Khan Academy over the years.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.930
We would not be able to
do what we're doing now

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.400
if not for the many years of support.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.360
And we're still running at a deficit,

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:21.760
so whether you're a corporate partner,

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.150
or even a small donor who can give $3, $5,

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.230
everything makes a difference
so that we can continue

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:30.800
to accelerate what we're offering.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.520
With that, I'm really eager
to introduce our guest,

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.880
Chancellor Richard
Carranza is the chancellor

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.350
of the New York City Public Schools,

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.970
the largest school district in America.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.090
And obviously the school
district in a part of the country

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.730
that has had unusual
challenges as we've gone

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.270
through this crisis.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.910
Chancellor Carranza,
thanks so much for joining.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.850
- Sal, thank you so
much for the invitation.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:54.100
I'm really honored to be here,

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.070
and I wanna say hi to everybody,

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.960
I'm sure many of our students
are watching as well,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.760
because they love what you do.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.110
So thank you for the invitation.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:03.990
- No, no.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.500
And so what I'm fascinated by is,

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.130
I would love, what was
going on in your life?

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:14.390
You are a New Yorker, and
obviously you're the head

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.440
of the largest school
district, highly complex

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.810
school district, in the country.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.690
What was going on in your
head in, what was it,

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.380
the end of March when it was clear

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.980
that we had a pandemic
in the United States,

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.340
it started to become clear that New York

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.590
was getting hard hit,
and people were at least

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.660
starting to surface the
notion of school closures?

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.723
What was going on in your mind?

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:38.000
- Well we had been preparing
for that eventuality

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.370
back, end of February.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.330
And one of the things that pernicious

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:45.750
about this pandemic and this virus

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.270
is that it's had so many iterations.

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.230
It keeps on iterating,
and it looks different.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:52.860
But we were trying to be prepared.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.240
So when the decision was made on March 16

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.610
that we would transition
to remote learning,

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.360
the one thing that we kept in mind

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.090
was that school was not closing.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:02.810
We were gonna continue learning,

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:06.000
but it was just not gonna be
in a face-to-face environment.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.230
And that's why we say we've
been in remote learning mode.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:11.690
Ever since then, it's been just a matter

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:13.530
of making sure that our teachers,

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.920
and our students, and our
families, quite honestly

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.770
because caregivers, parents, guardians,

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.290
grandmothers, neighbors,
they've all kind of become

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.710
new teachers as well, and
supports for their students.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:28.640
So we're making sure that
the entire organization

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.280
is really pivoted to developing resources,

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.510
providing resources,
and supporting students

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.110
and families in remote learning.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.590
- And I wanna remind everyone,
if you have questions

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:41.740
for Chancellor Carranza or myself,

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.590
feel free to post your questions
on Facebook and YouTube,

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:47.480
where this is streaming,
and we have team members

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.090
who are gonna be able to
surface questions to us.

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:53.270
Chancellor Carranza, in the week or two

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:55.793
before March 16, when the schools closed,

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:01.380
what was the order of operations?

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.530
There's so many schools,
I think it's 1.2 million

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.590
students you have, how did y'all

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:07.960
think about internet access?

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.800
How did you think about school lunches,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.720
special needs students?

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.130
How did you juggle all of that?

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.850
- So I have an incredible team of folks

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:20.840
that I work with that had already started

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.670
doing the logistical
planning around those issues,

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.970
but obviously we were
very concerned about,

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.680
if you remember during that time period,

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.860
even the medical professionals
were learning new things

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.760
about how this virus
was promulgating itself

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.340
in the community.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.710
So we were trying to stay up-to-date,

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.750
because school was still in session.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:42.007
And following the advice
of our medical experts,

00:04:42.007 --> 00:04:45.100
we were very much into protocols

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.400
about if there's a self-reported case,

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.090
if there's a confirmed
case, what is the protocol?

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:51.300
Do we shut down the school?

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.350
How do we notify families?

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.550
But as we became more and more apparent

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.610
that we were gonna go
into remote learning,

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:00.220
then the focus was really about

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:01.600
our most vulnerable students,

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.700
students with disabilities,
our multi-lingual

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.690
learner students, our immigrant students,

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:08.610
students in temporary
housing, which is a term

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.560
that we use in New York
City for homeless students,

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.180
and all of our vulnerable populations.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.090
At the same, we were also very concerned

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:20.300
about the fact that New York City,

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.530
almost nine million
residents in New York City,

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:26.580
and it was very clear that
the emergency responders,

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.840
our medical personnel,
our essential workers,

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.130
really depend on the
schools for their students

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:34.360
to be taken care of.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:37.460
So at the same time as we were planning

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:39.440
how to transition to remote learning

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:41.600
and shut the physical buildings down,

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:45.930
we were also standing
up over 150 what we call

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:49.620
regional enrichment centers, REC sites,

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.000
all over the city of New
York and every borough,

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.980
specifically for the
children of first responders,

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:57.130
medical personnel, and essential workers,

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:59.320
so that they would have,
they could still continue

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.440
to serve the residents of New York City

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.700
and know that their children
would be taken care of.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:05.550
That was a surreal experience,

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:10.000
because now you have children
in a school environment,

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.027
so all of the social distancing,

00:06:12.027 --> 00:06:14.470
and the face masks, and the precaution,

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:19.130
and having 24/7 cleaning
happening all the time,

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:20.610
and how do you serve the food?

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:22.820
All of those were the things
that we were talking about

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:24.310
and planning for.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:28.450
In addition, we know that
over 80% of the students

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:29.630
in New York City's public schools

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:31.260
qualify for free or reduced lunch.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:34.020
So the poverty is real.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:38.240
We know that many of our students
get their meals at school,

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:40.860
so at the same time we planned for,

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:45.260
and stood up over 450
meal hubs across the city,

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.200
in every borough, where students can come

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:50.350
get breakfast, lunch and
dinner all in one fell swoop.

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:53.160
And then about three weeks ago we extended

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.270
that to not only
children, but any resident

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.780
can come to a meal hub and
get all three meals as well.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:01.310
So we stood that up as
well because we know

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:05.350
that it's important for
students, and their parents,

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:06.550
not to go hungry.

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:08.490
I'll tell ya this Sal, right now,

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:11.070
since we transitioned March 16,

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:15.770
we have served almost nine
and a half million meals

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:19.760
out of those food hubs, and
we're averaging right now

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:23.060
a little over half a million meals per day

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:24.570
that are being distributed.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:26.360
And then we're also doing other things.

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.017
We heard from some of
our students that said,

00:07:28.017 --> 00:07:30.277
"You know, during the school year,

00:07:30.277 --> 00:07:32.587
"we get feminine hygiene products

00:07:32.587 --> 00:07:35.260
"at our school for free, and
there's a need for that."

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:38.450
So we've started now distributing
feminine hygiene products

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:39.520
at these meal hubs.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:42.710
Again, trying to be responsive
to the needs of our community

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:45.183
in making sure that they're
being taken care of.

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:47.900
- That's incredible, I can't even imagine

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:51.660
the complexity of doing
all of that so quickly.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.450
And we have a lot of comments coming in.

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:56.467
One, just from Facebook, David Wang says,

00:07:56.467 --> 00:07:58.537
"Thanks for taking care of the homeless

00:07:58.537 --> 00:08:00.540
"and those who don't speak English."

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:04.270
TL Kwo from Facebook says
"REC sites, fantastic idea."

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:05.190
And they're asking what it's like,

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:06.690
you kind of described it already.

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:09.400
It seems like it's a
hard, and I hadn't even

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.230
thought about that dimension of the crisis

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:12.530
until you just talked about it,

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:14.460
that yes, these healthcare workers

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.620
need some type of support.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:20.890
There's a question here
from Leslie Eldegril

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.647
from YouTube, "I work
for the valuable cause

00:08:27.647 --> 00:08:30.457
"of education in Georgia's
Kennesaw State University,

00:08:30.457 --> 00:08:32.247
"what can educator prep programs do

00:08:32.247 --> 00:08:33.657
"to support what you believe will be

00:08:33.657 --> 00:08:35.160
"the new norm Chancellor Carranza?"

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.010
And I'll add a question.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:39.480
So there's the short-term,
how did you navigate that,

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.390
where you had to go
whatever you wanna call it,

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:44.390
remote learning, distance
learning, in fairly short order?

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:45.560
What were the expectations?

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:46.550
How did you do training?

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:49.040
And then, what do you think
is, to Leslie's question,

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:50.990
what do you think is going
to be the new normal,

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:52.023
if there is one?

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:55.950
- Great questions, so thank you
for both of those questions.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:57.770
So let me give some context.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:01.160
So when we decided that
we would close schools,

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:05.330
we were fortunate that we
had three days to prepare

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:06.430
for remote learning.

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:09.287
And people are gonna
say, "What do you mean

00:09:09.287 --> 00:09:10.120
"you had three days?"

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:14.570
Well in essence, we took a
system of over 150,000 teachers,

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:17.000
1.1 million students, and
in a matter of three days,

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:21.210
72 hours, we completely
transitioned to remote learning.

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:23.750
Now we know that many of our teachers

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:28.200
were already experienced
with, and had done work with,

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:31.020
virtual learning, remote
learning, online learning,

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.370
blended environments, flipped classrooms,

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:35.870
really incredible work
here in New York City.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:38.240
But we also know that there was a modicum

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:41.130
of our teachers that had
never had that experience.

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:44.090
So it was a crash course,
a bootcamp if you will,

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:47.220
those three days, to
make sure that teachers

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.510
had their devices, knew where to log in.

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:53.730
We created something in our department

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:57.490
learning management
system called TeachHUB,

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:01.930
where we've put links, a link
to Khan Academy's in there,

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:04.850
and lots of resources for teachers

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:07.540
to teach remotely from home.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:11.020
So we stood that up, we
did intensive training

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:12.810
for those three days, and then we went

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:14.470
into remote learning mode, and the schools

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:16.060
have been closed, except for the ones

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:17.590
that are functioning as food hubs,

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:19.720
or these REC centers.

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:21.540
So for us, it was really making sure

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:24.080
that the educators had what they needed.

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:26.910
What was also important was to recognize

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:29.320
that not all of our students had devices.

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:31.480
So immediately, for those three days,

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:35.330
schools assigned the school-based laptops

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:39.160
and devices to their
students, again prioritizing

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:40.590
the most vulnerable students first,

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:42.460
students that needed those devices,

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:44.390
so students went home with those devices.

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:48.080
There are about 175,000 of those devices.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:49.760
But what we immediately did as well

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.260
is we worked with whoever could actually

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:54.810
produce for us, and that
happened to be Apple.

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:59.810
So we put in an order for
300,000 WiFi-enabled iPads.

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:02.550
And I'm happy to tell
you, and we did a survey

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:05.650
and we had principals
and teachers in contact

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:07.340
with their students, they were giving us

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:09.720
that information as to who needed devices.

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:13.100
And remember, these are 1800
schools in New York City,

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:15.940
so lots of information flowing to us.

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:19.640
But I'm happy to report
that as of this week,

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:23.270
we've now delivered, shipped and delivered

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:27.560
over 290,000 devices that are in the hands

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:29.580
of our students right now.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.860
So short-term versus long-term,

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.790
the short-term is making
sure that our students

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:36.847
had those devices,
making sure our teachers

00:11:36.847 --> 00:11:38.930
had the support that
they needed to transition

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:41.520
to remote learning, but the long-term

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.940
implications are vast and profound.

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:46.070
And this gets to the
question that came in as well

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:47.480
about teaching programs.

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:51.070
A crisis is always an opportunity
to do something better.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:53.920
And we, in education, have lamented

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:56.200
the digital divide for decades.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:59.200
This has forced us, across the country,

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:01.280
to really take on the digital divide.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:04.470
And if there's any silver
lining with this pandemic,

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:06.230
it's that when we get to the other side

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:08.740
of this pandemic, we will have bridged

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:10.600
the digital divide in New York City.

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:14.290
That's an amazing, amazing accomplishment.

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:16.480
But I think it's an
important accomplishment.

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:20.320
Now what that means is that
teacher preparation programs,

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:22.850
we're never gonna go
back to the way it was.

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:26.560
So technology and being
able to find resources

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.260
and utilize resources in different ways,

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:32.470
the ability, using
technology, to personalize,

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:35.240
even more so, the
learning needs of students

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:37.810
in a very individualized way.

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:39.820
We are seeing that
there's tremendous power

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:42.390
right now, in the hands of
teachers, to be able to do that.

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:46.020
So if I was a professor
in a teaching university,

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.730
preparing teachers, I would be insisting

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:53.660
that we have lots of pedagogy
for this flipped classroom,

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:56.470
online teaching, virtual teaching,

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:59.570
that teachers have the
wherewithal to be able to use

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:02.040
Google Classroom, or to
be able to use the Zoom,

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:05.001
or to be able to use
Microsoft Teams, or WebEx,

00:13:05.001 --> 00:13:08.740
because it'll provide
them with additional tools

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:12.180
in their pedagogical toolkit to be able

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:17.180
to continue this trend that
students will be learning

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:21.710
in virtual mode, at least
some segment of their day.

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:24.350
And it provides us with a
lot of other opportunities.

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:25.710
I'll give you just one more example,

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:27.180
sorry for going on and
on, but I'll give you

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:28.013
one more example.
- No, no, no,

00:13:28.013 --> 00:13:29.430
this is super interesting.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:30.810
- So when you think about the fact

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:34.455
that in AP classes,
advanced placement classes,

00:13:34.455 --> 00:13:35.950
(Richard clears throat)
in New York City,

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:40.950
excuse me, in New York City,
we have over 700 high schools,

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:44.370
we have some very small high schools

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:47.170
that may not have the ability
to have an AP teacher for,

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:49.030
let's just say AP statistics.

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:54.030
So what we piloted this year
was a virtual remote AP class,

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:57.490
a bunch of AP classes, and the notion was

00:13:57.490 --> 00:14:00.160
that if we have a very well trained,

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:03.590
very capable AP teacher,
let's say in statistics,

00:14:03.590 --> 00:14:06.010
and they're at their
school doing their job

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:07.760
and doing a great job, but we know there's

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:09.548
five other schools that have students,

00:14:09.548 --> 00:14:13.070
one student at one school, two
students at another school,

00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:16.510
three at another, if we can
set up that virtual classroom

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:19.690
with that teacher,
providing AP instruction,

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:22.160
and then those students
remotely, at those other schools,

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:24.510
are able to tap into that teacher

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:26.550
and get their AP
instruction, would it work?

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:28.740
Well it was working gangbusters

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:30.520
before we went into remote learning.

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:31.353
Guess what?

00:14:31.353 --> 00:14:33.660
That has provided us
now with the opportunity

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:36.187
to think about, "Okay, how do we leverage

00:14:36.187 --> 00:14:38.287
"the real skillset that we have embedded

00:14:38.287 --> 00:14:41.617
"in our teachers, and provide
those learning opportunities

00:14:41.617 --> 00:14:44.697
"to more and more students,
especially in students

00:14:44.697 --> 00:14:47.007
"in communities that have
been historically underserved,

00:14:47.007 --> 00:14:51.437
"and especially in small
schools where you don't

00:14:51.437 --> 00:14:53.547
"necessarily have a lot of teachers,

00:14:53.547 --> 00:14:55.327
"but you still have students that wanna

00:14:55.327 --> 00:14:56.960
"have those experiences?"

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:59.010
Those are the kinds of experiences

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:02.370
that I think have
presented themselves to us

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.090
as a response to this crisis.

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:07.120
And I think that we have a responsibility

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:10.160
not to lose momentum once we get back

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:11.590
to in-person learning.

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:13.540
In-person learning will look different.

00:15:14.630 --> 00:15:15.950
- Now I love that last example,

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:18.900
because it really shows how
there's maybe a silver lining

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:20.845
that maybe these teachers are able

00:15:20.845 --> 00:15:23.320
to go beyond the confines of
their individual classrooms,

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:25.210
and it's kind of like the whole district

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:26.750
is serving the whole district,

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:28.900
which is really, really powerful.

00:15:28.900 --> 00:15:30.980
Tons of questions and
comments from Facebook,

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:34.647
Shelly Fox says, "Thank
you to the New York City IT

00:15:34.647 --> 00:15:36.140
"device deployment team."

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:37.250
I actually relate to that.

00:15:37.250 --> 00:15:38.957
You just kind of nonchalantly said,

00:15:38.957 --> 00:15:41.467
"Yeah, we deployed 290,000 laptops,

00:15:41.467 --> 00:15:43.710
"and we've essentially
solved the digital divide."

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:45.340
Exactly how did that happen?

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:46.910
'Cause we've talked to a lot of districts,

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:48.780
obviously to leverage
Khan Academy you need

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:51.130
device access, internet access.

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:53.800
I am curious about the
broadband aspect of it,

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:56.118
and then just how did you pull that off?

00:15:56.118 --> 00:15:58.300
There's other superintendents
who might listen

00:15:58.300 --> 00:15:59.830
to this and they would love to be able

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:01.760
to emulate what you did.

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:04.580
- So I wanna give tremendous kudos

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:06.530
to our device deployment office as well,

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:10.710
to our purchasing and
procurement office, to our mayor.

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:14.430
So very early on when we
knew that this device issue

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:17.890
was going to be an issue,
we immediately jumped

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:20.690
into action mode and we contacted,

00:16:20.690 --> 00:16:25.120
we put out calls to see who could actually

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:28.970
handle the volume that
we were going to need,

00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:30.997
and Apple stepped right up and said,

00:16:30.997 --> 00:16:33.407
"We will make sure that
we get you what you need

00:16:33.407 --> 00:16:35.580
"in record time," and
they kept their word.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:37.490
We also worked with IBM that helped us

00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:40.690
with the installation
of the WiFi capability,

00:16:40.690 --> 00:16:44.580
then we worked with FedEx
and UPS to develop a protocol

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:45.760
of how these would be shipped,

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:48.090
because obviously if we were then under

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:51.210
a stay at home order, and
we're trying to limit contact,

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:53.830
the last thing you wanna do
is having people come out

00:16:53.830 --> 00:16:55.110
to have to get their devices.

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:58.890
So all of those partners were incredible

00:16:58.890 --> 00:17:00.590
at making sure that they prioritized

00:17:00.590 --> 00:17:03.340
getting these devices into
the hands of students.

00:17:03.340 --> 00:17:06.410
And then what we also did is made sure

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:09.120
that we had a way of
surveying our students

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:12.990
and our teachers to find those individuals

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:15.280
that maybe hadn't raised their hand

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:17.610
and said, "I need a
device," so that we weren't

00:17:17.610 --> 00:17:19.000
leaving anything to chance.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:20.780
And as those devices started coming in,

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:23.250
again, they were prioritized for

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:24.840
our most vulnerable populations.

00:17:24.840 --> 00:17:27.640
We went from there and
we expanded outward.

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:30.920
We still have devices so
that if, there may be a case

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:32.660
where a student had their own device

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:34.650
and now it's broken, they can request one

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:36.283
and we'll ship it right to them.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:39.930
It was a massive undertaking, but we had

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:41.630
some incredible colleagues here

00:17:41.630 --> 00:17:42.790
in the Department of Education

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:45.000
that just jumped right into action.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:46.560
One of the things that we also did

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:50.250
with these devices is that
we didn't want any family

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:53.310
to feel that they
couldn't request a device

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:54.347
because "what if it breaks?

00:17:54.347 --> 00:17:56.207
"Am I responsible for it?

00:17:56.207 --> 00:17:57.330
"I'm not working."

00:17:57.330 --> 00:18:00.410
So what we did is every device comes,

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:02.840
right out of the box
it comes WiFi enabled,

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:04.790
they get a hard case with it,

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:06.470
and we also purchased insurance

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:07.450
for every single device.

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:10.260
So our students and
parents don't have to worry

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:11.770
if something happens.

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:13.800
We wanted them to have the device as well.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.740
And you know, in New
York, we sometimes get

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:19.880
a little blase about
the size of the numbers,

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:22.540
but you're right, 290,000 devices,

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:25.123
it's as if you took Seattle and you took,

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:29.440
I think in Seattle, Detroit,
San Francisco and Boston,

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:31.350
and put 'em all together,
and we just provided

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:32.970
all of those systems with devices,

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:35.680
that's kind of what
the team was able to do

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:37.373
in pretty record time.

00:18:38.230 --> 00:18:39.660
- And how are you all tackling,

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:41.130
and that's amazing, first of all,

00:18:41.130 --> 00:18:43.900
and how are you tackling
the broadband issue?

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:47.440
Have the telecom carriers
helped out there?

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:50.430
- Yes, so our telecom
providers have been great,

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:55.430
they've really stepped up
and provided WiFi access

00:18:56.020 --> 00:18:58.590
for our students, especially our students

00:18:58.590 --> 00:19:00.510
that live in temporary
housing, or students

00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:04.150
that are in our public housing units.

00:19:04.150 --> 00:19:05.950
That was the first place that they went in

00:19:05.950 --> 00:19:08.970
and made sure that there
was WiFi available.

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:10.970
We are in discussion with them now,

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:13.810
obviously when we first went
into remote learning mode,

00:19:13.810 --> 00:19:15.300
which was in March, the plan was

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:19.100
that we would come back to
face-to-face learning in April.

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:21.690
Well obviously that didn't happen,

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:24.270
we're finishing the
academic year through June

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:25.520
in remote learning.

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:29.590
Summer learning will be in
remote learning mode as well.

00:19:29.590 --> 00:19:31.580
And as I've mentioned,
our goal is to be back

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:35.150
in in-person learning September,

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:40.090
but again, the medical
experts are gonna dictate

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:42.520
how fast we come into in-person learning.

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:44.780
So I have the entire organization focused

00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:48.450
on remote learning through the
rest of this calendar year,

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:50.510
just to be prepared.

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:53.730
So we are in conversations now

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:56.510
with all the carriers about extending

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:59.700
that Wifi access and looking
at what that would look like.

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:02.590
But I have to say that,
in times of crisis,

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:05.730
you really see the true
nature of individuals

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:08.930
and organizations, and
I just cannot say enough

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:13.360
about what our WiFi
providers have done about,

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:15.810
I've mentioned some of
the shipping companies,

00:20:15.810 --> 00:20:17.930
I've mentioned some of
the technology companies,

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:22.930
but a lot of partners that
provide online resources

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:28.100
and online materials
have really stepped up

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:31.730
in a very unprecedented way
to support our children.

00:20:31.730 --> 00:20:34.080
And I'd like to say to them, "Thank you,"

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:36.930
because without their assistance,

00:20:36.930 --> 00:20:40.200
I know that we would not be as successful

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:43.221
with it as I think we are.
We've got a lot of work to do,

00:20:43.221 --> 00:20:44.580
we're definitely building the plane

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:46.870
as we fly the plane, but
we couldn't have done it

00:20:46.870 --> 00:20:49.640
without these partners and businesses,

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:51.030
so I just wanna give them a big public

00:20:51.030 --> 00:20:52.720
shout out and thank you.

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:53.760
- No, definitely.

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:55.270
What you just described
over the last five,

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:57.090
that's one of the best
stories I've heard, frankly,

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:58.580
in the last couple of weeks.

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:00.560
And obviously I've had
a lot of conversations

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:01.990
with folks about the digital divide

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:05.080
and device access, and
that really gives me hope

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:05.913
for where we can go.

00:21:05.913 --> 00:21:08.130
And to your point, not
just through this crisis,

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:11.100
but post-crisis, hopefully
the digital divide

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:15.120
will be a smaller divide, or
hopefully even get solved.

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:17.400
You touched on summer and back to school,

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:18.650
how do you think summer's going to be

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:19.940
different this year?

00:21:19.940 --> 00:21:21.570
Historically things like summer programs

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:22.810
would be for a subset of kids,

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:24.850
maybe the kids who might've
fallen behind a bit.

00:21:24.850 --> 00:21:26.757
Do you think it's going to be,

00:21:26.757 --> 00:21:28.237
"Hey, everyone needs to just keep learning

00:21:28.237 --> 00:21:30.080
"through the summer?"

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:33.920
- Well I think one thing
I don't think we speak

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:35.900
enough about, and it's
critically important,

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:39.550
is that the children, and
everyone in New York City,

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:41.210
but everyone in the world, and definitely

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:43.590
in the United States, New York city

00:21:43.590 --> 00:21:46.470
was the epicenter of the
epicenter in the United States,

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:47.980
which was New York State.

00:21:47.980 --> 00:21:49.750
So we were in the
epicenter of the epicenter,

00:21:49.750 --> 00:21:50.760
we've been hit hard.

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:55.060
We've lost colleagues,
everybody knows somebody

00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:58.943
that has been lost due
to this coronavirus-19.

00:21:59.990 --> 00:22:03.460
So we can't forget the
trauma that our students,

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:06.060
and our staff, and our
community has undergone.

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:07.994
It's a traumatic event.

00:22:07.994 --> 00:22:12.820
And we are starting to see
some of the signs of the trauma

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:16.450
with students, and students
not being able to sleep.

00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:21.450
So I saw this also when
I was superintendent

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:24.500
in Houston and we were
hit by Hurricane Harvey,

00:22:24.500 --> 00:22:26.930
and I remember seeing the
manifestation of trauma

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:29.000
and how that affected
people in different ways.

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:31.180
And the same thing is happening with this.

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:34.120
So as much as I want
students to continue to learn

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:36.230
and always to be learning, I also think

00:22:36.230 --> 00:22:38.020
that the first opportunity that we have

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:41.080
for students to just breathe,
that's gonna be good.

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:42.970
We've embedded social-emotional learning,

00:22:42.970 --> 00:22:46.640
trauma-informed curriculum,
trauma-informed supports

00:22:46.640 --> 00:22:50.530
in all of the materials,
in all of the resources

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:52.400
that we've provided for teachers.

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:55.950
We also have a parent
hub, where parents have,

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:58.500
not all of them, but they
have a lot of resources

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:01.170
that they can use to
work with their students,

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:04.220
their children, and help
support their children

00:23:04.220 --> 00:23:05.350
as they work through it, and they can

00:23:05.350 --> 00:23:06.700
work through it as well.

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:09.380
But I think that the
trauma-informed approach

00:23:09.380 --> 00:23:11.160
is gonna be critically important

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:14.120
as we come back to in-person learning.

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:16.690
As a result of that, I also think

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:21.230
that summer, we're gonna
prioritize our vulnerable students.

00:23:21.230 --> 00:23:24.860
Obviously students with
disabilities will be prioritized

00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:25.940
over the summer.

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:29.030
We've had remote learning
plans for every one

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:30.920
of our students with disabilities

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:34.410
that has adapted their
individual education plan

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:37.000
for a remote learning environment.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:39.960
We also instituted,
through a lot of feedback,

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:42.100
but we instituted a new grading policy,

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:44.780
because you can't, a new
attendance policy as well,

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:46.699
you can't shove the old attendance policy

00:23:46.699 --> 00:23:48.920
into a remote learning environment,

00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:50.510
it just wouldn't work.

00:23:50.510 --> 00:23:52.180
So we have a new attendance policy,

00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:53.840
we also have a grading policy,

00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:57.300
and in essence, students
are continuing to work,

00:23:57.300 --> 00:23:59.830
they're continuing to turn in assignments,

00:23:59.830 --> 00:24:02.800
but instead of failing,
no student's gonna get

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:04.870
a failure at the end
of this academic year,

00:24:04.870 --> 00:24:07.490
what they'll get is an in progress grade.

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:09.890
And what the in progress grade signifies

00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:11.710
is that they have a
little bit more work to do

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:13.460
to finish the requirements of the class,

00:24:13.460 --> 00:24:15.310
or finish the requirements of the grade,

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:16.690
you know third grade or fourth grade.

00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:19.040
And then those students
will be prioritized

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:21.430
for summer learning in the remote learning

00:24:21.430 --> 00:24:23.310
environment as well.

00:24:23.310 --> 00:24:27.110
And the whole point is to
give students more time.

00:24:27.110 --> 00:24:29.737
And even there I would just say this,

00:24:29.737 --> 00:24:32.267
"The goal as an educator, for me,

00:24:32.267 --> 00:24:36.097
"has always been that students
master the subject matter,

00:24:36.097 --> 00:24:37.990
"that they get mastery."

00:24:37.990 --> 00:24:39.620
I'm not necessarily wanting students

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:40.920
to go for the A, the B, the C,

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:44.200
I want them to master what
they're trying to learn.

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:47.100
So this has forced us
now to really embrace

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:49.400
this notion of mastery learning.

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:52.480
So if the student hasn't
mastered that subject matter,

00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:53.880
they're not gonna get a failure,

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:56.757
they're gonna get in
progress, and that says,

00:24:56.757 --> 00:24:58.247
"You have some more work to do,

00:24:58.247 --> 00:25:00.160
"we have some more work
to do to support you,"

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:03.150
because the goal is not
that you finish first,

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:04.860
but that you finish.

00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:07.640
And that, again, is changing the narrative

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:11.550
for how we look at,
and how we give weight,

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:13.800
to what grades really are.

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:15.890
So anyway, that's how we're prioritizing

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:17.900
for summer learning.

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:20.670
And I'll tell ya that
many of the organizations

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:23.000
and institutions in New York City,

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:25.832
the museums, the Park and Rec Department,

00:25:25.832 --> 00:25:29.160
the libraries, are really working with us

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:32.270
to be able to create, even if
it's some virtual experiences

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:34.230
that students can have
over the summer as well.

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:36.710
So it's not just reading,
writing and arithmetic,

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:39.880
but they also have some other
experiential experiences

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:41.680
that they can engage in, even if it's in

00:25:41.680 --> 00:25:43.743
a virtual way, over the summer.

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:47.040
- Wow, and just so you
know, you're getting

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:49.290
a lot of praise on social media.

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:51.507
Peggy says, "Chancellor
Carranza, what a great leader.

00:25:51.507 --> 00:25:52.797
"Really getting this from every angle,

00:25:52.797 --> 00:25:54.350
"it's rare to find people
with these abilities."

00:25:54.350 --> 00:25:57.640
And I think that we can
all sense the authenticity.

00:25:57.640 --> 00:25:59.112
We have a little bit of time left.

00:25:59.112 --> 00:26:02.560
What do you see as the
scenarios for back to school?

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:05.660
Obviously there's the two
extremes where we stay

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:08.498
fully remote, or we go
completely back to normal,

00:26:08.498 --> 00:26:11.040
but what are the other scenarios
that you're contemplating?

00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:13.730
And how do you think things
might be changed forever

00:26:13.730 --> 00:26:15.540
because of this?

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:18.220
- Well, again, we're
always gonna be driven

00:26:18.220 --> 00:26:19.950
by what the medical experts tell us.

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:23.000
So we're gonna listen to the science.

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:25.610
As a parent, I would not want my child

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:27.120
to go back to a school environment

00:26:27.120 --> 00:26:29.280
unless I was confident that my child

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:32.570
was gonna be safe,
secure, and not infected.

00:26:32.570 --> 00:26:34.240
So there's a number of things

00:26:34.240 --> 00:26:37.940
that are beyond our control as educators,

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:39.700
and my control as a chancellor.

00:26:39.700 --> 00:26:41.960
And so we're gonna listen
to the medical experts

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:43.945
and make sure that we have the protocols

00:26:43.945 --> 00:26:46.784
and the materials, the
testing that we need,

00:26:46.784 --> 00:26:51.784
and the precautionary gear
that we'll need as well.

00:26:51.990 --> 00:26:55.823
But we've actually been
modeling a number of scenarios.

00:26:56.660 --> 00:26:59.340
I'll just give you an example of a couple.

00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:01.960
And these are by no
means do I mean to say,

00:27:01.960 --> 00:27:03.990
this is exactly what's gonna happen.

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:06.190
It's gonna be driven by
whatever the circumstances

00:27:06.190 --> 00:27:08.600
are at the time that the
decision has to be made

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:10.860
about going back to
in-person learning or not.

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:12.570
But I'll give you an example.

00:27:12.570 --> 00:27:14.590
So when we come back, it's pretty obvious

00:27:14.590 --> 00:27:15.920
that we're gonna have to probably still

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:18.140
practice social distancing.

00:27:18.140 --> 00:27:19.870
How do you practice social distancing

00:27:19.870 --> 00:27:23.650
in the very, very
tight-knit, tight quarters

00:27:23.650 --> 00:27:25.970
that are New York City's public schools?

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:28.500
We're a very compact city.

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:30.530
So almost nine million residents,

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:32.550
how do you do that in our schools?

00:27:32.550 --> 00:27:34.780
Well, so if you have an elementary school,

00:27:34.780 --> 00:27:37.600
for example, does that mean
then that you have shifts?

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:39.190
So you have an early morning shift,

00:27:39.190 --> 00:27:41.330
and part of your students
comes in the early morning,

00:27:41.330 --> 00:27:42.820
and then you have an afternoon shift,

00:27:42.820 --> 00:27:44.070
and the other half of the students

00:27:44.070 --> 00:27:47.300
come to that part of the learning?

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:49.610
And then how do you
split teachers' schedules

00:27:49.610 --> 00:27:51.910
to cover both shifts?

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:53.300
That could be one thing.

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:55.790
Or do you do something where it becomes

00:27:55.790 --> 00:27:58.070
a little bit more of a remote learning?

00:27:58.070 --> 00:28:00.560
So for example, you take
that same elementary school

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:02.310
and you do a cohort model.

00:28:02.310 --> 00:28:04.210
So you take all of the students,

00:28:04.210 --> 00:28:07.260
you have cohort one, two, and three.

00:28:07.260 --> 00:28:12.010
Cohort one goes to school
while cohorts two and three

00:28:12.010 --> 00:28:14.380
practice remote learning on Monday.

00:28:14.380 --> 00:28:16.750
On Tuesday, cohort two goes to school

00:28:16.750 --> 00:28:20.340
while cohorts one and
three are remote learning.

00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:22.270
On Wednesday, cohort three goes to school

00:28:22.270 --> 00:28:25.380
while one and two are
practicing remote learning.

00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:27.220
That's only because you wanna provide

00:28:27.220 --> 00:28:30.500
the distance to do social distancing.

00:28:30.500 --> 00:28:31.940
What would it look like?

00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:34.720
We know that the CDC has talked about,

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:36.890
as part of what their recommendations are,

00:28:36.890 --> 00:28:39.880
is that students don't
congregate in lunch rooms

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:41.610
whenever they get back
to in-person learning.

00:28:41.610 --> 00:28:44.690
That they'll eat their
lunch and their breakfast

00:28:44.690 --> 00:28:45.560
in the classroom.

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:46.900
So what does that look like then?

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:47.830
And how do you deliver it?

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:48.663
How do you serve it?

00:28:48.663 --> 00:28:50.180
How do you clean up after it?

00:28:50.180 --> 00:28:51.593
What does the day look like?

00:28:52.447 --> 00:28:53.630
What do buildings look like?

00:28:53.630 --> 00:28:55.650
Are there buildings that
we are just not gonna

00:28:55.650 --> 00:28:58.260
be able to use because
they're just physically

00:28:58.260 --> 00:29:01.730
not equipped to handle
the social distancing

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:03.610
that may be required?

00:29:03.610 --> 00:29:07.120
All of those questions, and
a myriad of other questions

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:09.390
are things that, right
now, we're grappling with

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:12.310
and modeling to see how
that would look like.

00:29:12.310 --> 00:29:13.460
But it gives you a little insight

00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:16.030
to the complexities that all educators,

00:29:16.030 --> 00:29:18.420
and all of my colleagues,
and our system's leaders

00:29:18.420 --> 00:29:20.923
are really grappling right now with.

00:29:22.380 --> 00:29:23.980
And we're doing it in an environment

00:29:23.980 --> 00:29:27.250
that we don't know what the ground floor

00:29:27.250 --> 00:29:29.420
is gonna look like when we get to June,

00:29:29.420 --> 00:29:30.870
and July, and August.

00:29:30.870 --> 00:29:32.030
We just don't know.

00:29:32.030 --> 00:29:34.110
I'll give you one other example of how

00:29:34.110 --> 00:29:37.240
the ground is shifting
right beneath our feet.

00:29:37.240 --> 00:29:40.390
Two weeks ago, nobody was talking about

00:29:40.390 --> 00:29:44.770
PMIS, which is a pediatric
multi-system syndrome,

00:29:44.770 --> 00:29:46.713
which I think is Kawasaki disease.

00:29:47.800 --> 00:29:49.200
Children are getting very, very sick.

00:29:49.200 --> 00:29:50.573
In New York City, we
have over 100 students

00:29:50.573 --> 00:29:54.170
that are being treated for that syndrome.

00:29:54.170 --> 00:29:56.840
And we know that doctors are exploring,

00:29:56.840 --> 00:29:59.980
is there a relationship
between COVID-19 exposure

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:01.950
and then this syndrome?

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:04.060
Two weeks ago we weren't
even talking about that.

00:30:04.060 --> 00:30:08.057
So that's how rapidly this
is manifesting and iterating.

00:30:09.200 --> 00:30:12.400
This virus is iterating,
so the sand is definitely

00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:14.310
shifting beneath our feet.

00:30:14.310 --> 00:30:16.500
So what we're trying
to do is model a number

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:20.020
of scenarios and be prepared so whatever

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:23.280
those circumstances are,
we can pivot very quickly

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:25.500
to a model, or at least we've thought

00:30:25.500 --> 00:30:27.840
about what some of these
implications would be

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:29.350
as we make those decisions to go back

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:31.280
to in-person learning.

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:32.220
- Wow.

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:35.680
Well Chancellor Carranza,
given all that you've done,

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:36.700
one it's heartening, and as I said,

00:30:36.700 --> 00:30:38.780
there's a lot of good news embedded in,

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:41.120
obviously, the larger not so good context

00:30:41.120 --> 00:30:42.050
that we are all dealing with,

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:43.640
especially that y'all are
dealing with in New York.

00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:46.110
But I think I speak for everyone watching

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:47.550
that you'll keep doing the amazing work

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:49.200
that you and your team are doing.

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:51.790
It gives confidence in
folks to see leadership

00:30:51.790 --> 00:30:54.883
like what you're providing,
and I'm honored, frankly,

00:30:54.883 --> 00:30:56.100
that you're able to make a little bit

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:57.800
of time for us today,
because I can imagine

00:30:57.800 --> 00:31:00.100
there's many, many things
going on in your world.

00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:01.890
So thank you so much for joining us

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:04.950
and I hope we can do this
again in a few weeks.

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:06.450
- I would love to do it, and thank you

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:07.320
for the invitation.

00:31:07.320 --> 00:31:09.800
And to everybody that's out
there watching, listening,

00:31:09.800 --> 00:31:11.760
stay safe, take care of each other.

00:31:11.760 --> 00:31:14.240
This is a time for us to
take care of each other.

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:16.540
So thank you for the
opportunity, and be safe.

00:31:17.410 --> 00:31:19.070
- Thank you.

00:31:19.070 --> 00:31:21.170
Well thanks everyone for joining,

00:31:21.170 --> 00:31:23.400
I hope you were as inspired as I was.

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:25.760
I think in times of
stress and times of crisis

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:28.500
that people show their true colors,

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:30.160
and I think you're seeing examples

00:31:30.160 --> 00:31:33.260
of really great leadership
from Chancellor Carranza,

00:31:33.260 --> 00:31:34.690
and from many others that we've had.

00:31:34.690 --> 00:31:36.803
And it's heartening to see how folks

00:31:36.803 --> 00:31:39.910
have been stepping up
to do the right thing.

00:31:39.910 --> 00:31:43.730
Look forward to joining
you on future livestreams.

00:31:43.730 --> 00:31:46.100
I will see all of y'all tomorrow.

00:31:46.100 --> 00:31:47.450
Stay safe and stay healthy, just like

00:31:47.450 --> 00:31:48.850
Chancellor Carranza told us.

